    "description": "The history of Iranian flags includes military standards, royal banners, and later state designs whose functions and meanings differed. Literary accounts, visual depictions, and legal descriptions provide valuable evidence, but they do not support every precise reconstruction circulated today.",
    "tags": ["Historical flags", "Military standards", "Derafsh Kavian", "Lion and Sun", "Historical evidence"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:21:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:21:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Historical Flags of Iran: Banners, Evidence and Change | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Read the evidence for Iran's ancient standards, the legendary Derafsh Kavian, and later flag traditions, with reconstruction limits explained."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "functions-before-designs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with what a banner did",
        "content": "A historical flag gallery can make every dynasty appear to have possessed a single, fixed national design. The evidence requires more care. Iranian banners served military, royal, and ceremonial purposes, and different emblems could be used concurrently. The Persian word derafsh could describe a guiding or signaling device: its visibility helped troops locate a commander and rally during battle. [s1]\n\nThis practical function explains the importance of height, conspicuous colors, and shimmering materials in literary descriptions. A banner was both something to recognize and something to follow. Its association with an army or ruler does not automatically make it equivalent to a modern flag representing an entire population. Identifying its function is therefore as important as identifying its pattern. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ancient-standards",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ancient standards and incomplete images",
        "content": "Alireza Shapur Shahbazi's survey brings together classical texts and visual evidence for ancient Iranian standards. It describes separate standards for Achaemenid army divisions and cites Xenophon for a royal golden eagle mounted on a shaft. These references indicate a differentiated system of recognition rather than one design used for every purpose. [s2]\n\nImages can be harder to interpret than modern reconstructions suggest. The damaged bird on the standard in the Alexander Mosaic has been identified differently by scholars, including as an eagle or a cock. A responsible caption should preserve that uncertainty. A modern drawing that completes the damaged image is an interpretation of evidence, not a surviving ancient flag. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kavian-tradition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Derafsh Kavian: legend and historical argument",
        "content": "The Derafsh Kavian is associated with the royal standard of the Sasanians. In the Shahnameh's legendary account, Kaveh, a blacksmith, raises his leather apron on a spear during a revolt against the tyrant Zahhak. After victory, the ruler Fereydun enriches it with precious decoration; succeeding kings add further jewels. This is an origin story conveyed through epic tradition. [s3]\n\nDjalal Khaleghi-Motlagh notes that the standard is not directly named in the Avesta or in Achaemenid or Parthian sources. Scholars have nevertheless proposed different accounts of its antiquity and development. The distinction is essential: the tradition's cultural importance is well documented, while claims about its earliest appearance or exact reconstruction require qualification. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "later-state-symbols",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Later emblems and changes of state",
        "content": "The Lion and Sun was established as a recognized emblem of Persia by the time of Shah Abbas, but attributing it to every earlier Safavid banner is unsafe. Shahbazi identifies contrary evidence and warns about suspect flag illustrations. His survey also documents varied early Qajar uses and later standardization. These changes should be examined through dated examples rather than projected backward as an unchanged design. [s4]\n\nFollowing the revolution of 1979, the state's emblem changed. The constitutional description retains green, white, and red while specifying the Islamic Republic's emblem and motto. This establishes the prescribed elements, but a complete account of the transition also needs implementation records and design standards. Those remain a research requirement for this page. [s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-flag-evidence",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How to distinguish evidence from reconstruction",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Historical banner claim\"] -->|\"Check evidence type\"| B[\"Written description\"]\n  A -->|\"Check evidence type\"| C[\"Visual depiction\"]\n  B -->|\"Identify date and function\"| D[\"Bounded historical interpretation\"]\n  C -->|\"Check damage and attribution\"| D\n  D -->|\"Separate conjectural details\"| E[\"Clearly labeled reconstruction\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This editorial method distinguishes descriptions and depictions from reconstructed designs. The arrows describe research steps, not a historical succession of flags.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
